= Ideation <sec-ideation>

== Initial ideas on the table and why they were considered

Two main directions were on the table. The first was our earliest concept: a blockchain-based model using Graph Neural Networks to monitor cryptocurrency transactions for compliance with EU transparency regulations, making ownership and transaction data verifiable and tamper-proof, with a roadmap to later extend to traditional bank transactions. It was considered because market research indicated a theoretical need for advanced transaction monitoring given the scale of illicit crypto activity, and early signals suggested potential clients existed.

The second direction, which became ComplAI, was an AI-native diagnostic tool to help early-stage FinTechs and SMEs navigate complex European regulation, drawn from discovery conversations. Founders across sectors consistently shared the same frustration: their biggest drain of time and budget before launch was not building the product, but handling compliance.

== Selection criteria and method used to narrow down

To evaluate both ideas, we applied a feasibility and market-barrier assessment grounded in customer discovery and expert interviews, prioritizing real feedback over our own assumptions to test whether a lean team could build and sell each idea quickly.

Testing the crypto monitoring concept revealed two major obstacles. Domain experts, such as Niklas Ego at the Deutsche Bundesbank, noted that established, heavily funded players dominate the space. More critically, buyer interviews showed banks rarely invest in advanced detection unless under direct threat of sanctions --- a barrier an early-stage startup cannot realistically break into.

Applied to the ComplAI concept, the same filter produced a clearly different result: interviews with founders, former bankers, and legal experts confirmed a clear pain point and real willingness to pay, and a diagnostic tool required a far lower barrier to entry than a real-time institutional monitoring system.

== Why this idea was chosen over the alternatives

We chose to pursue ComplAI over the original crypto concept for two main reasons. First, current solutions are cost-prohibitive for our target segment, since building the necessary technology requires substantial capital and lengthy timelines. Second, this pain point was directly confirmed through our own market validation, and the barrier to entry proved far lower than competing against entrenched institutional vendors in transaction monitoring. Together, these factors made the shift from a technical, institution-facing idea to a founder-facing diagnostic platform the clear strategic choice.
